<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Çankırı</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gangra, Germanicopolis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Çankırı is a city in northern central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. In antiquity, as Gangra, it was the inland seat of the kings of Paphlagonia and later a Roman city renamed Germanicopolis. The Council of Gangra was held there in about 340. It passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> after 1071 and became an Ottoman provincial centre, known for its rock salt mines.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cankiri.jpg" alt="Çankırı" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: XperfectTR · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_view_of_%C3%87ank%C4%B1r%C4%B1.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Çankırı lies about 140 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>, some 800 metres above sea level. Its ancient name Gangra seems to mean a goat in Paphlagonian. The earlier town stood on the hill behind the modern city, where the ruins of a late fortress remain, while the Roman city occupied the site of the present one.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Gangra seems once to have been the capital of Paphlagonia, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deiotarus-philadelphus">Deiotarus Philadelphus</a>, the last king of Paphlagonia, lived there, though he also calls it only a small town and a garrison. It was absorbed into the Roman province of Galatia on the king's death in 6 or 5 BCE. It was later named Germanicopolis, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a> or possibly the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>. In Christian times it was the metropolitan see of Paphlagonia, and its bishop Hypatios, venerated as a saint in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> tradition, was killed by Arians on his return from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">Council of Nicaea</a> in 325.</p><p>In the 4th century Gangra was the scene of an important synod, whose date is disputed. Twenty-one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> met to act against Eustathius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Sebaste</a> and his followers, who disowned marriage and denounced riches. The synod issued twenty canons, declared ecumenical by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-chalcedon">Council of Chalcedon</a> in 451, and said it condemned not virginity but the dishonouring of marriage, and not poverty but disparagement of honest wealth. In 1400 and 1401 the patriarch Matthew I appointed George Kontophe metropolitan of Gangra, at the request of its people, who were threatened by the Turkomans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Çankırı is the seat of Çankırı Province and of Çankırı District, with about 90 thousand inhabitants. Wheat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, beans and apples are grown in the surrounding fields, and Çankırı Karatekin University was founded there in 2007.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Çankırı” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Paphlagonians lived between Bithynia and Pontus, along the Black Sea coast and the forested mountains behind it, with <b>Gangra</b> as an inland seat.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-paphlagonia">Kingdom of Paphlagonia</a></li></ul></section>
